use std
into surf.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with sf_ is exported). It analyzes the display triangle mesh of a CAD part: surface curvature from the exact vertex normals, the draft angle of every vertex against a pull direction with a classification of every CAD face, and zebra stripe coordinates for reflection-line continuity checks. The mesh splits its vertices along face boundaries, so every vertex belongs to exactly one CAD face. Its only import line is `import math;`, ending with a semicolon. Every int used in real math is converted with `as real`, and a real used as an int with `as int`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls.

```amni
extern fn sf_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "sf_view_f64";
extern fn sf_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "sf_view_i32";
```

Module state. Ints, all starting at 0: `NV` (vertex count), `NT` (triangle count), `NG` (CAD face count). Growable f64 arrays: `P` (3 per vertex: x, y, z), `N` (3 per vertex: unit normal), `VAL` (1 per vertex: the analyzed value), `TV` (1 per vertex: display coordinate from 0 to 1), `UV` (2 per vertex: texture coordinates), `HV`, `KV`, `W` (1 per vertex: mean curvature, Gaussian curvature, area weight), `ST` (24 statistics), `FS` (4 per CAD face). Growable i32 arrays: `IX` (3 per triangle: corner vertex indices), `TF` (1 per triangle: its CAD face index, or minus 1), `FC` (1 per CAD face: its class).

Statistics in `ST`: 0 the largest convex principal curvature, 1 to 3 the x, y and z of the vertex where it occurs, 4 the largest concave principal curvature (as a positive number), 5 to 7 the x, y and z of that vertex, 8 the smallest mean curvature, 9 the largest mean curvature, 10 the smallest Gaussian curvature, 11 the largest Gaussian curvature, 12 the display scale used for `TV`, 13 the total surface area, 14 to 17 the area of the faces in draft classes 0 to 3, 18 to 21 the number of faces in draft classes 0 to 3, 22 the smallest draft angle in degrees, 23 the largest draft angle in degrees. Draft classes: 0 positive draft, 1 requires draft, 2 negative draft, 3 straddle (the face has both positive and negative draft).

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` does the same for an i32 growable array, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. On the web an array that holds entries cannot grow past the room it first reserved, so every array is emptied before it is sized again.

The exported function `sf_views()`, declared with the export keyword, registers the arrays JavaScript reads or writes through sf_view_f64 and sf_view_i32: f64 views 1 `P`, 2 `N`, 3 `VAL`, 4 `TV`, 5 `UV`, 6 `ST`, 7 `FS`, 8 `HV`, 9 `KV`; i32 views 10 `IX`, 11 `TF`, 12 `FC`. It returns 0.

The exported function `sf_setup(nv, nt, ng)`, declared with the export keyword, takes three i32 values (vertex, triangle and CAD face counts), sets `NV`, `NT` and `NG` to them, sizes with the helpers `P` and `N` to 3 nv zeros, `VAL`, `TV`, `HV`, `KV` and `W` to nv zeros, `UV` to 2 nv zeros, `ST` to 24 zeros, `FS` to 4 ng zeros, `IX` to 3 nt zeros, `TF` to nt entries of minus 1 and `FC` to ng zeros, then calls `sf_views` and returns nv.

`tri(t, o)` takes an i32 triangle index t and an f64 growable array o by reference holding at least 10 entries. With a, b and c the corner vertices IX[3t], IX[3t plus 1] and IX[3t plus 2], e1 the vector from vertex a to vertex b and e2 the vector from vertex a to vertex c (positions in `P`), it writes into o: entry 0 the triangle area (half the length of the cross product of e1 and e2), entries 1 to 3 the unit face normal (the cross product divided by its length, or zeros when that length is at most 1e-30), entries 4 to 6 the unit vector along e1 (zeros when e1 has length at most 1e-30), and entries 7 to 9 the cross product of the unit face normal and that unit vector. It returns 0.

The exported function `sf_curv(kind, expo, contrast)`, declared with the export keyword, takes an i32 kind (0 curvature magnitude, 1 mean curvature, 2 Gaussian curvature) and two reals, and estimates the curvature at every vertex from how the vertex normals change across each triangle. It sets `HV`, `KV` and `W` to zeros (all entries), entries 0 to 13 of `ST` to zero, and every entry of `FS` to zero, and makes a local f64 array o of 10 zeros. For each triangle t from 0 to `NT` minus 1 with corners a, b and c as in `tri`: it calls `tri`(t, o) and skips the triangle when o[0] is at most 1e-30; it adds o[0] to ST[13]; with u the vector o[4], o[5], o[6] and v the vector o[7], o[8], o[9], it starts nine local reals m00, m01, m11, m12, m22, r0, r1, r2 at zero and, for each of the three edges (from a to b, from b to c and from c to a, each with the edge vector the position of the second vertex minus the first and the normal change the normal `N` of the second vertex minus the first), takes eu and ev the dot products of the edge vector with u and v and du and dv the dot products of the normal change with u and v, and adds eu times eu to m00, eu times ev to m01, eu times eu plus ev times ev to m11, eu times ev to m12, ev times ev to m22, eu times du to r0, ev times du plus eu times dv to r1 and ev times dv to r2. The symmetric 3 by 3 matrix has rows (m00, m01, 0), (m01, m11, m12) and (0, m12, m22); its determinant is det; with tr equal to m00 plus m11 plus m22, the triangle is skipped when the absolute value of det is at most 1e-12 times tr times tr times tr. Otherwise the second fundamental form entries l, m and n solve that system with right side r0, r1, r2 by Cramer's rule, the triangle mean curvature is hm equal to l plus n, halved, and its Gaussian curvature is gk equal to l times n minus m times m; then, with w equal to o[0] divided by 3.0, each of the three corners gets w times hm added to `HV`, w times gk added to `KV` and w added to `W`. After all triangles, for each vertex i with W[i] above zero, HV[i] and KV[i] are divided by W[i]. Then for each vertex i: hm is HV[i], gk is KV[i], sq is the square root of the larger of 0.0 and hm times hm minus gk, k1 is hm plus sq and k2 is hm minus sq; when k1 is above ST[0], ST[0] becomes k1 and ST[1] to ST[3] the position of vertex i; when minus k2 is above ST[4], ST[4] becomes minus k2 and ST[5] to ST[7] the position of vertex i; ST[8] and ST[9] track the smallest and largest hm and ST[10] and ST[11] the smallest and largest gk, starting from the first vertex's values; and VAL[i] is the larger of the absolute values of k1 and k2 when kind is 0, hm when kind is 1, and gk otherwise. For each triangle t whose face g equal to TF[t] is at least 0 and below `NG`, the face statistics are updated from its corners: FS[4g] becomes the largest k1 and FS[4g plus 1] the largest minus k2 seen at the face's corners (both computed per corner from HV and KV as above, starting from zero), and FS[4g plus 2] gets the triangle area added. The display scale hi is the largest absolute value in `VAL` divided by contrast (when contrast is at most zero, divided by 1.0), or 1.0 when that is not above zero; ST[12] is hi. For each vertex i, with a equal to the absolute value of VAL[i] divided by hi, raised to the power expo, and capped at 1.0, TV[i] is a when kind is 0, and otherwise 0.5 plus 0.5 times a when VAL[i] is at least zero and 0.5 minus 0.5 times a when it is negative. UV[2i] and UV[2i plus 1] are 0.5. It returns `NV`.

The exported function `sf_draft(dx, dy, dz, req)`, declared with the export keyword, takes four reals: a pull direction (made unit length, or 0, 0, 1 when its length is at most 1e-12) and the required draft angle in degrees. For each vertex i: s is the dot product of its normal from `N` with the pull direction, clamped to minus 1.0 and 1.0; VAL[i] is the arcsine of s converted to degrees (times 180 divided by pi), so 90 is a face looking along the pull direction, 0 a wall parallel to it and a negative angle a face looking away; TV[i] is 0.4 when VAL[i] is at least req, 1.0 when VAL[i] is at most minus req, and 0.6 otherwise; UV[2i] and UV[2i plus 1] are 0.5. ST[22] and ST[23] are the smallest and largest VAL, starting from the first vertex. ST[13] to ST[21] start at zero. For each face g, FS[4g] starts at 1e30, FS[4g plus 1] at minus 1e30 and FS[4g plus 2] at zero. With a local f64 array o of 10 zeros, for each triangle t whose face g equal to TF[t] is at least 0 and below `NG`: it calls `tri`(t, o), adds o[0] to FS[4g plus 2] and to ST[13], and lowers FS[4g] to and raises FS[4g plus 1] to the VAL of each of its three corners. Then for each face g whose FS[4g plus 2] is above zero, its class c is 0 when FS[4g] is at least req, else 2 when FS[4g plus 1] is at most minus req, else 3 when FS[4g plus 1] is at least req and FS[4g] is at most minus req, else 1; FC[g] is c, FS[4g plus 3] is c as real, ST[14 plus c] gets FS[4g plus 2] added and ST[18 plus c] gets 1.0 added. Faces with no area keep FC equal to 0 and FS[4g plus 3] equal to minus 1.0. It returns `NG`.

The exported function `sf_zebra(vx, vy, vz, ux, uy, uz, count)`, declared with the export keyword, takes seven reals: the viewing direction (from the eye into the scene), the stripe axis (the screen up direction) and the stripe count. Both directions are made unit length (left as they are when their length is at most 1e-12). For each vertex i with normal n from `N`: dn is the dot product of the viewing direction and n, the reflected ray r is the viewing direction minus 2.0 times dn times n, s is the dot product of r and the stripe axis clamped to minus 1.0 and 1.0, UV[2i] is count times the sum of the arcsine of s divided by pi and 0.5, UV[2i plus 1] is 0.5, and TV[i] is 0.5. It returns `NV`.

`main()` does nothing.
